Kevin Wayne <killedbyafoo@yahoo.com> writes:
> The real issue isn't farming or extinctionism. It's that many people
> have complained that the later game (post-Castle pre-ascension run) is
> *boring,*

That is a legitimate complaint, and one with which I largely agree.

> and it's boring largely because there's nothing to prevent, or even
> to dissuade, players from screwing around buffing up their
> characters to the point where nothing in the game offers any
> challenge anymore.

If anyone is bored with nethack because of a lack of challenge, I'd
suggest they explore the voluntary conducts, alone or in combination.
Anyone claiming that, e.g., the "inventoryless" conduct is
unchallenging had best be prepared to post some convincing ttyrecs.

Also, the "screwing around" of which you complain is largely necessary
precisely because of the "sprint" section already in the game. The
ascension run is a matter of random, cheap and unpredictable drains on
resources, with no chance to stop and recover. You either vastly
overprepare, or you trust your fate to the RNG. Skill matters, but no
matter how good you are you have to count on getting mysterious-forced
endlessly and getting just the wrong inventory items cursed at just
the wrong time.

> This "screwing around" includes farming and extinctionism, and
> you'll notice that people who engage in them don't talk about what a
> challenge the game is at that point, but how mindlessly tedious it
> is.

Which would seem to make these things their own punishment, nyet?

I think P-farming is a tedious bore, so I don't do it. But I fail to
see how someone else doing it makes my own gameplay any less
interesting. It does tend to make obvious the silliness of the
current scoring system, but that's more a symptom than a cause. (If
you want to get lots of points, there are plenty of things that give
you more points in fewer turns with less overall risk.)

> The suggestion for a soft in-game "clock" is merely a suggestion to
> keep the game challenging throughout. The hostility that this
> suggestion engenders is astonishing.

I dislike that suggestion chiefly because it is a drastic change to
the game as a whole, and one which breaks many (non-abusive) styles of
play which I and others happen to enjoy. To the extent that
post-castle boredom is really a problem at all, there are plenty of
ways to address it that are far less intrusive. (I'd like to see more
variety -- perhaps some new nasty monsters that can give kitted
players a scare, or new special levels.)

In short, the source of the "hostility" is that the new game you're
proposing to put in nethack's place would be a joyless chore for me to
play.


